// Practice quiz: DTOs and mapping
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-data-jpa/dtos-and-mapping

::o1 request DTO::What belongs in a request DTO for registering a user? {
~Every entity field#Not quite. That invites mass assignment.
~The database id, so the client chooses it#Not quite. The server assigns it.
=Only the fields a client may set#Correct. Everything else stays server-controlled.
~The password hash#Not quite. The server computes it.
}

::o1 response DTO::Why use a response DTO rather than the entity? {
=It exposes only what the contract promises#Correct. Internal fields cannot leak by accident.
~Records serialize faster#Not quite. Speed is not the point.
~JSON requires DTOs#Not quite. JSON has no such rule.
~Entities cannot have getters#Not quite. They usually do.
}

::o2 one place::Why keep entity-to-DTO mapping in one place? {
=A contract change has one home#Correct. Duplicated mapping drifts.
~It runs in parallel#Not quite. Parallelism is unrelated.
~Spring requires a mapper bean#Not quite. Spring has no such requirement.
~It removes the need for tests#Not quite. Mappers still deserve tests.
}

::o3 lazy risk::How can returning an entity from a controller run extra SQL? {
~Controllers always run SQL#Not quite. A plain controller runs none.
~JSON writing commits the transaction#Not quite. That does not issue the extra selects.
~The response header runs a query#Not quite. Headers do not.
=Serialization calls getters that trigger lazy loading#Correct. The JSON writer walks properties.
}

::o3 leak risk::An entity has a passwordHash getter and is returned directly. What can happen? {
~The hash is encrypted first#Not quite. Nothing encrypts it.
=The hash appears in the JSON response#Correct. Serialization includes visible properties by default.
~It is removed by JPA#Not quite. JPA does not touch JSON.
~The request fails#Not quite. It usually succeeds, which hides the leak.
}

